From 7b86deaed8aa80b30c0fc61dbdf40524ec4b15c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 16:59:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a toggleable post hoc outlier cap on the fODF

Removes isolated glyphs whose amplitude is pathologically large, without
touching orientation and without keying on tissue type. A voxel is flagged
when its peak exceeds 10^orders times the MEDIAN peak of its in-mask
neighbours, or exceeds an absolute ceiling, and is then scaled down to
min(neighbourhood median, ceiling). Strictly one sided: a voxel is never
raised, so this can remove spurious amplitude but can never invent fibre
density.

The neighbourhood test is what makes this different from every per voxel
weight tried before it. It is relative, and edema is spatially contiguous, so
an edematous voxel's neighbours are edematous too and the ratio does not move.
A whole region being uniformly bright or dim never trips it; only isolated
spikes do. That is a structural property of the statistic rather than a
well-tuned threshold, and it is verified directly: a contiguous block raised
by a uniform factor is never flagged while isolated spikes in the same volume
always are.

Three details that are easy to get wrong and are handled explicitly. The
scale factor is not target/peak: plm is stored with p_00 = 1, so the isotropic
floor is not part of the blow-up and the correct factor is
(T - 1/4pi)/(peak - 1/4pi) applied to the l>=2 block. The statistic is a
median, not a mean and standard deviation, because an SD has no breakdown
point and blown-up voxels come in contiguous clusters. And the whole l>=2
block is scaled by one scalar, which preserves the peak vertex exactly.

The ceiling defaults to 1, not the smaller values that look reasonable.
Measured at Lmax 6: the hard physical max of a band-limited fODF is 2.228, a
true single fibre peaks at 1.459, SMI recovers 0.84-0.86, and 60-64% of
simulated WM voxels exceed 0.5. A ceiling of 0.4-0.5 would flatten most of
the white matter. 1 is an empirical ceiling and the report says so: it is only
safe because SMI under-recovers the high l bands.

Note that with the shipped regularization the simulation has no outlier
population at all (worst CSF voxel 1.7x the median WM peak), so Ncap = 0 is
the expected result and a non-zero count on real data is itself the finding.

Adds SMI.fODF_OutlierDefaults, SMI.cap_fODF_outliers and
SMI.neighbour_median, the options.fODF_outlier parsing and call site, log
entries, README section, REPORT_fODF_outlier_cap.md, and two test harnesses
that both pass. out.plm, out.pl and out.kernel are bit identical whether the
flag is on or off. Exactly one existing line changed, so that the modulation
sees the corrected fODF when both are enabled; a fit that does not enable the
cap is unchanged.

